# Native FP16 arithmetic audit: machine code preserves the fused norm

The original C32 FP16 cubin and the existing `norm_pair`/`norm32_value` helpers
use the same normalization sum: channels 16–31 are squared and rounded to Half,
then channels 0–15 are squared inside a Half FMA with those rounded values.
The remaining Half reduction tree also matches. **No separate FP16 normalizer
is justified by this audit.** Active implementation and native probe files were
not changed; the three uncompiled separate-square helper drafts were removed.

The initial PTX-only conclusion was wrong. Its `mul.f16x2` and `add.f16x2`
instructions compiled to `HMUL2` plus `HFMA2`. The CPU witness is retained as an
example of an unfused interpretation that does not describe the executable.
Deployment's independent review prompted the SASS check before any activation.

The dataflow work below is CPU-only. Root subsequently ran the unchanged
guarded native FP16 probe with the installed v24 extension under memcheck:
**all 16 cases passed byte-exactly**, covering four fixtures and four phases
at 16×24×32, plus 48 ordinary and 48 poisoned CUDA-graph replays. Memcheck
reported zero errors. This establishes bounded ordinary-C32 numerical parity,
not FP16 trunk completeness, timing or DLL-host execution.

The evidence is `outputs/vendor_fp16_c32_v24.json` and
`outputs/vendor_fp16_c32_v24_memcheck.log`. The unchanged helper SHA is
`63ac3709bc76cd7217a22837414ab4be7b7fb73826d94bb6df47c71b4c641c2d`;
the v24 extension SHA is
`baab87033c6725bd298db2d42fb00a48f2d19290471574ade2dc3a6ff9c9cf98`.
Fixtures are identity residual, FFN basis, attention basis and derived real
block-1 weights. Native allocations and provided candidate inputs/weights
have guards; candidate results use extension allocations and are retained and
byte-checked. The derived Half record is not a separately shipped FP16
checkpoint or proof of the DLL host's conversion.

## What the C32 machine code proves

`tools/fp16_norm_sass_audit.py.draft` traces the original ordinary function
`cc_tinlayout_fused_swin_1h_32_1` in the pinned module-0 cubin. It follows QKV
weight vector offsets, both K16 MMAs and their accumulator registers, then
Half multiply/FMA/add, butterfly shuffles, register moves and low/high swaps.
Unknown destinations are invalidated. Each norm-floor consumer must equal the
existing helper's expression tree. Only commuting one two-operand add is
allowed; the proof never reassociates rounded additions.

The check covers **all 128 Q/K vectors**, 64 Q and 64 K, and **32 half2 FMAs**.
For every vector, the fused square is channel `i` and the separately rounded
square is channel `i+16`, for `i=0..15`. The resulting sum reaches the same Half
floor as the candidate. The output is
`outputs/fp16_norm_sass_audit.json.draft`.

| Pinned asset | SHA256 |
|---|---|
| Module-0 cubin | `feb368ff5279a7408b1e55554db6e468d7f114a24b18b2af8d7e6989a410c612` |
| Module-0 cached SASS | `27002afb7279c924b40a727a75db45f053d2da4860a80178c79252162333ef83` |
| Extracted function SASS | `81790db74a47958c71d4625a2897e589a9956f9b6c8f40730c0a95b1832431ae` |
| Original PTX function | `edbc95312c9200a4405f5cfa58dbd86511e1a21eae953020aab85843dae7c4e0` |

A concrete Q example uses first-K16 B vectors at record offsets `0x4060`
(channels 0–15) and `0x4260` (16–31), then `0x4c60`/`0x4e60` for the second
K16. The final raw N8 fragments for the first M16 tile are R32/R33, R40/R41,
R146/R147 and R144/R145. At PC `0x4ef0`, `HMUL2 R19,R146,R146` squares channels
16/17 plus the subgroup channel offset. At `0x4f10`,
`HFMA2 R19,R32,R32,R19` fuses channels 0/1 into that sum. The adjacent N8 pair
uses `0x4f00`/`0x4f30`, then `HADD2` combines them at `0x4f60`.

A K example uses `HMUL2 R12,R140,R140` at `0x51c0` and
`HFMA2 R16,R6,R6,R12` at `0x51d0`. Weight-to-MMA tracing establishes these as
K channels 16/17 and 0/1 respectively. The adjacent pair is fused at `0x5160`.
The script checks every later token tile, including the delayed Q reductions
around PCs `0x7cb0`–`0x8020`; it does not infer the whole function from these
examples alone.

## Exact matching sum and the disproved PTX witness

Let `H` round to Half. The executable and candidate both compute

```
p[i] = H(x[i]*x[i] + H(x[i+16]*x[i+16]))  # one binary16 FMA
p = H(p[:8] + p[8:16])
p = H(p[:4] + p[4:8])
p = H(p[:2] + p[2:4])
sum = H(p[0] + p[1])
```

The native C-fragment implementation expresses the same tree with two
adjacent channels per word, XOR-2/XOR-1 butterfly shuffles and a final
low/high Half add.

`tools/fp16_normalization_audit.py.draft` retains the earlier unfused PTX
interpretation under explicit names. With `x[0]=0.5400390625` (`0x3852`),
`x[16]=0.2022705078125` (`0x3279`) and every other channel zero:

| Interpretation | Half sum |
|---|---:|
| Unfused PTX squares, each rounded before addition | 0.332763671875 (`0x3553`) |
| Existing candidate and native SASS fused expression | 0.33251953125 (`0x3552`) |

Thus a forced separate-square helper would change the currently matching
arithmetic. The witness is not a native mismatch or a reason to modify
FP16/FP8 selection. CPU reciprocal-square-root values in the report are
nominal mathematical results, not an emulation of the GPU approximation.

## Other ordinary C32 checkpoints

| Checkpoint | Finding |
|---|---|
| Norm floor | Both clamp to Half-rounded 6.2e-5, exactly 6.198883056640625e-5. Native SASS uses `HMNMX2`; the candidate converts the Half sum to float and applies the same exactly represented floor. |
| Norm inverse | Native uses `MUFU.RSQ` following Half-to-float conversion, then packs back to Half before multiplication. Candidate source uses `rsqrtf` and the same Half publication. Exact native output still needs the guarded GPU check. |
| Head scale | Native loads float at record offset 30816 and explicitly converts it to Half before multiplying already normalized Half Q. PTX module line 238031 and SASS `F2FP.F16.F32.PACK_AB` at `0x59c0` show this conversion. The candidate's Half scale matches; real block-1 scale becomes 0.40869140625. |
| Window exp | Both use Half FMA constants 0.044921875 and 1.30078125 and clamp to [1.03125,1.5693359375]. The native packed shift/add `((word << 5) + 0x7ff88000)` equals the candidate's independent Half shifts for **all 304704 clamped pairs**. |
| Denominator | Both sum adjacent N8 fragment pairs, then four pair sums sequentially, subgroup partials in order 0/1/2/3, then low+high. The CPU shuffle proof checks all 32 rows in the first half-window; the second half has the same pattern. |
| Denominator inverse | Native uses `rcp.approx.ftz.f32` then Half conversion; candidate source uses `1.f/value` then Half conversion. This remains an approximation check for native execution, not a demonstrated Half-bit difference. |
| FFN residual | Both seed the first contraction MMA with Half input times Half FFN scale. Compare the candidate's default skip=input contract; the ordinary native entry has no external skip argument. |
| Attention residual | Native `%r3226/%r3227` derive from raw FFN Half times Half attention scale and seed the first projection MMA. The candidate retains raw FFN and uses the same seed. |
| Publications | FP16 expansion activation, contraction, raw QKV, Q/K, V, probability, attention and final output remain Half. No E4M3 publication is introduced. Candidate raw/published output are identical in FP16. |
| Cubic activation | Half clamp, two Half FMAs and final Half multiply match for bounded finite fixtures. This audit does not certify nonfinite payload behavior. |

These checks do not replace the separate activation/weight address proof,
replay/immutability tests or original-kernel execution. Tensor-core arithmetic,
complete output equality and speed are outside the CPU proof's scope.

## Wider and global scope

The C64/C128/C256 and global QKV PTX entries also show separate-looking squares.
Those opcode counts are retained in the first audit report **only as PTX
observations**. They cannot establish executable rounding.

Deployment independently mapped global `cc_vit_1d_qkv` SASS. For Q, final
MMA registers R76/R74/R72/R70 represent channels 0/8/16/24, then partition
adds place these in R12/R14/R16/R18. `HMUL2` at PCs 0x3930/0x3950 rounds the
high-channel squares, and `HFMA2` at 0x3970/0x3990 fuses low-channel squares.
For K, final MMA registers R68/R66/R64/R62 become R36/R38/R40/R42 after the
partition adds. PCs 0x5540/0x5550 square the high channels, and 0x5560/0x5570
fuse the low channels. XOR-2, XOR-1 and low/high swap-add preserve the same
tree. These are dynamic register versions: the partition additions must be
included when following reused registers. This confirms the global norm
grouping, but does not validate its different completion/accumulation protocol.

## Call sites reviewed; no changes proposed

| Existing call site | FP16/FP8 behavior |
|---|---|
| `block32.cuh::block32_kernel` | Calls paired `norm_pair`; FP16 shared path and FP8 shared baseline share the matching fused sum. |
| `window_attention.cuh::window_attention_kernel` | Calls paired `norm_pair`; serves wider FP16 attention and the shared FP8 path. |
| `global_attention.cuh::global_attention_kernel` | Calls scalar `window_inference::norm` for Q and K; delegates to `norm32_value`. |
| `global_prepacked.cuh::prepare_global_qkv_kernel` | Calls scalar `norm` for Q/K, then preserves the existing two-stage global Q scaling. Vector/prefetch attention consumes this preparation and does not renormalize. |
| `attention_math.cuh::norm32_kernel` | Calls scalar `norm32_value` for the existing `normalize32` API. |
| `model.py::Numerics.norm` | FP16/FP8 reference uses `_cosine_normalize`; extension uses `normalize32`. FP32/BF16 training takes the earlier smooth-normalization branch. |
| `block32_register.cuh`, `block32_rotated.cuh`, wider expert/cache/prefetch/QKV kernels | Their `normalize_c` is an FP8-only fragment helper; no FP16 contract is inferred from a Half accumulator. |

The public training path, FP8 code, model initialization and native helper stay
unchanged. The bounded C32 native probe now passes. Wider/global FP16 proof
requires its own guarded activation/weight layout and protocol validation;
isolate any actual difference before changing arithmetic or comparison criteria.
